WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.620
You know guys at its core, CS:GO is a tactical game.

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:09.600
In-game leaders and coaches spend countless hours coming up with a myriad of strats for each

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.420
and every single map.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:15.200
But sometimes it's the little stuff that can trip you up.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:19.040
Like not buying a defuser, or burning to death in your own molly.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:38.320
(Casting)

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:42.320
And so here we are with the top 10 misplays in CS:GO.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:49.180
Kicking off this list is team KKona’s inVert at CEVO Pro League Season 9.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:52.120
In a match that was already going to live
on in infamy

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:59.120
(Stream)

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:05.120
inVert found himself in a 1v2 situation with the bomb planted at B. While setting up his

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:11.040
defense, he tossed a molly onto window, and the rest is just… well, tragic.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:32.840
(Casting)

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:37.760
Coming in at number 9 is Team Liquid’s Koosta at ELEAGUE Season 1.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:44.240
In a tight 2v2 situation against C9, Koosta found himself with the bomb as time ticked away.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:50.300
All he had to do was plant it within 4 seconds, but then, this happened.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:02:12.420
(Casting)

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:17.820
Making its way in at number 8 is SK Gaming’s semifinal match against Ninjas in Pyjamas

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:20.940
at IEM Oakland 2017.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:26.400
Having found an early lead, the Brazilians looked to widen the gap even further

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.640
if only they had just defused the bomb.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:34.460
Let this be a lesson in just how critical communication is at Counter-Strike’s highest level.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:58.520
(Casting)

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:04.000
When you’re already down 13-2 at the half and 0-2 in the group stage, a team kill is

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:07.260
definitely not what you need to turn things around.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:12.040
That’s why CaptainMo of TyLoo earns our number 7 spot on this list.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:19.100
Taking on G2 at IEM Oakland 2016, TyLoo desperately needed something to rally around, but that

00:03:19.109 --> 00:03:21.760
thing was not a team kill.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:25.640
We could say more about this play, but we’re going to let Semmler take it from here.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:44.400
(Casting)

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:50.220
At the number 6 spot is Magiskboy, whose North squad were taking on SK Gaming at ESL One

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:52.860
Cologne 2017.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:59.080
Down 15-10 on Cache, Magisk had the perfect opportunity to extend the map by planting

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:01.580
the bomb after taking out TACO.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:08.960
Instead, this play serves as a PSA for just how big of a roll movement plays in a game of CS:GO.

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:24.680
(Casting)

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:30.720
It brings us no pleasure to inform you that number 5 marks Koosta’s second appearance

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:37.820
on this list, this time as a amember of Counter Logic Gaming at Dreamhack Valencia 2017

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:43.380
With CLG on the cusp of advancing to the playoffs, Koosta was the one tasked with the defuse

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:47.340
and everyone watching thought that everything was wrapped up.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:48.780
Except it wasn’t.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:53.700
Just take a look at FNS’ face, the man who actually had the defuse kit.

00:04:53.780 --> 00:05:08.800
(Casting)

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:12.640
Oh yeah, CLG ended up losing the series in overtime.

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:22.880
(Casting)

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:28.960
Coming in hot at number 4 is Cloud9 at ESL ESEA Pro League Season 1 Finals.

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:34.470
During the summer of 2015, the North American squad were on a tear, making three consecutive

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:39.240
grand finals and beating out multiple European
juggernauts in the process.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:43.000
But even when you’re the best from NA, you’re still NA.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:48.540
At the tail end of the sixth round on Overpass against Fnatic, Cloud9 just needed to defuse

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:50.070
the bomb to win.

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:54.240
But I have no words to describe what happened
next.

00:05:54.260 --> 00:06:16.560
(Casting)

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:21.860
Coming in at Number 3 is CajunB with Astralis at the MLG Columbus Major.

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:26.700
While the Danes were trailing in the first half of their semi final match against Natus Vincere,

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:33.560
Cajunb &amp; co got a lucky break when Na’Vi left themselves no time to plant the bomb.

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:36.220
All CajunB to do was stay alive.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:38.160
His reaction says the rest.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:56.980
(Casting)

00:06:56.980 --> 00:06:59.120
You had one job Cajunb.

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:00.560
One job.

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:06.080
Despite winning the ELEAGUE Atlanta Major, Xyp9x takes the runners up spot on our list

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:08.640
in a quarterfinal match against Na’Vi.

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:12.640
Na’Vi were on map point in round 30 of Game #2.

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:18.720
Being the clutch master that he is, Xyp9x waited patiently in a 1v2 and caught seized

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:21.420
and Edward right where he wanted them.

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:24.960
That is, if he hadn’t forgotten to line up his shots.

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:38.220
(Casting)

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:45.400
Hopping off the bomb at number 1 is Ex6tenz at ASUS ROG Winter 2015.

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:50.600
Down a map against NiP, Titan’s in-game leader was looking to bring the French squad

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:51.960
to match point.

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:58.440
And in a 1v1 against NiP’s Maikelele, all Ex6tenz had to do was hold the defuse.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:02.120
But with the pressure of the grand final, anything can happen.

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:33.980
(Casting)

00:08:33.980 --> 00:08:38.880
Well guys, that's our list for this week. Now if you didn't already know we put out great content each

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:43.600
and every week on this channel, and if you're not subbed yet. Well, that's the real misplay.

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:49.920
Thanks for watching. If you want more great content just like this, be sure to hit the subscribe button.

